# Task instruction — blind stage-log coding of a frame chunk

You are coding one **chunk** of a screen-recording frame sequence into a raw stage log.
This is observational coding, not analysis: you are producing the data that a later,
separate synthesis pass will read — not writing the synthesis yourself.

## 0. Hard constraints, before anything else

- **Do not read** any prior analysis, audit, session summary, codebook, or
  usability-findings document, and do not read any other participant's or scenario's
  stage log, if any of these are reachable to you. Code only from the frames you are
  given. If you are unsure whether a file in reach is "prior analysis," treat it as
  off-limits and say so in a note rather than opening it.
- **Do not read or use transcript/audio.** You are coding the screen only. Anything that
  would require audio (in particular, who is *speaking*) is a proxy at best — see FAC below.
- **One row per frame. Never skip a frame, never merge frames, never interpolate a value
  from a neighbouring frame.** If a value is not legible in *this* frame, the cell is `?`
  (present but unreadable) or `-` (not in viewport at all) — not a guess, not a carry-forward.
- **Open and inspect every frame individually at native resolution.** Where a value you need
  (a lane-header ECTS number, a dashboard figure, banner text) is small, crop and upscale
  that region before transcribing it. A thumbnail or contact-sheet pass is not sufficient —
  digits like 2/3, 3/5, 0/6/9 are genuinely ambiguous at reduced size and must be resolved
  at full resolution or marked `?`.
- **Corroborate every plan-state number before accepting it.** Cross-check each lane's ECTS
  reading against the dashboard's Planned-ECTS total and, where visible, the
  "Gesamtumfang: N ECTS fehlen bis 180" line. If a reading doesn't reconcile, say so
  in the row's `notes` and/or in `## ANOMALIES` rather than silently picking one value.
- Work through the chunk in timestamp order, frame by frame. Do not sample every Nth
  frame or skip ahead because a run "looks stable" — stability itself is a finding
  (record it explicitly, e.g. "identical to t-10") but only after you've looked.

## 1. Input

You will receive a directory (or archive) of frames for a single chunk: one image per
sampled timestamp, 10 seconds apart, filename encoding the timestamp
(e.g. `a_0470.jpg` = 470 seconds into this run's phase). A chunk covers a contiguous
range of one participant/scenario recording (e.g. `a_0000`–`a_0560`); the run may
continue in a sibling chunk file — you are not responsible for frames outside your
own range, but note in `## ANOMALIES` if your first or last frame looks mid-action
(e.g. a modal open with no visible start), so the boundary is flagged rather than silently
lost.

## 2. Output file

One Markdown file per chunk, named `<participant>-<scenario>_<first-ts>-<last-ts>.md`
(e.g. `P1-A_a_0000-a_0560.md`), containing, in order:

1. A title line and one italic provenance line: what produced this coding (pass
   type, sampling interval, taxonomy version), e.g.
   `*Blind pass, 10s sampling, taxonomy v1.0.*`
2. The frame table (format below).
3. `## ANOMALIES` — always present.
4. `## BRIEF` — always present if any `guide`-surface frame occurs in the chunk;
   omit only if the chunk contains no guide frames at all.
5. `## GRAPH USE` — present only if any frame in the chunk has `surface = graph`.
6. `## FINAL STATE` — present only if the chunk is the *last* chunk of its run
   (i.e. it ends the recording, or ends in `guide`/`quest` after the planner is
   closed for good). Omit for chunks that hand off mid-plan to a sibling chunk.

Use a plain `t | col | col | ...` pipe table (with or without a leading/trailing
`|` and separator row — either is acceptable) or fence it in a code block if the
row content contains characters that would break Markdown table rendering (long
quoted banner text with embedded `|`, for instance — escape those as `\|` instead
if you keep the table unfenced). Be consistent within one file.

## 3. Columns, exactly as follows

`t | surface | sidebar | panels | activity | lanes | ects | miss | warn | focus | banner | flags | notes`

### t
Seconds into the chunk's phase, taken directly from the filename. Integer.

### surface — exactly one, primary state of the screen
- `guide` — the study-guide/instructions app (brief, persona, "Studienplaner öffnen").
  Not the planning tool itself.
- `quest` — questionnaire steps inside the guide app (post-scenario ratings etc.).
- `auth` — sign-in / sign-up, or the planner's own Setup modal (programme, start term,
  graph toggle) shown before the Table/Graph canvas is usable.
- `load` — blank page / "Loading…" / unpainted.
- `table` — planner open, Table view (semester lanes).
- `graph` — planner open, Graph view (node-link canvas).
- `other` — browser chrome only, an unrelated tab/window, desktop — nothing of the
  study visible.

### sidebar — one of `cat` (Course Catalog) | `filt` (Filters) | `off` (hidden) | `n/a`
`n/a` when surface isn't `table`/`graph`. Note: Table view's sidebar defaults to the
Course Catalog; Graph view's defaults to Filters — record whichever is actually shown,
don't assume from the surface alone.

### panels — comma-separated, any that apply; `-` if none
- `recs` — Recommendations panel visible.
- `dash` — right-hand Planner Dashboard open. When open, append which collapsibles are
  currently **expanded** (not collapsed) in braces, using: `req` (Required courses),
  `focus` (Focus Area), `mods` (Required modules), `exam` (Per Exam Subject),
  `sem` (Per Semester), `hours` (Week-Hours), `cat$` (Per Category),
  `miss` (Missing Requirements), `warn` (Warnings). Example: `dash{mods,miss}`.
  Empty braces `dash{}` means the dashboard is open but every collapsible is
  collapsed (only the top summary tiles are showing).
- `legend` — Visual Legend overlay.
- `modal` — any other modal/popup: the onboarding tour, a course's semester-placement
  menu, the axis-config editor, a confirm dialog, etc. Say which, in `notes`.
- A one-off browser-chrome popup (autofill/password-manager prompts, etc.) is **not**
  an app `modal` — note it in `notes` instead and leave `panels` reflecting the
  app's own state.

### activity — best inference of what the participant is doing this frame
One of `read · drag · click · search · scroll · zoom/pan · idle · unclear`.
Base it on cursor position/shape, drag-ghost artefacts, a transient banner just having
fired, or a visible change from the previous frame. At 10 s sampling this is
inferable maybe 60–70% of the time — when your basis is weak (no cursor, no ghost, no
delta from the last frame), append `~` to flag low confidence (e.g. `read~`). Never
present a guessed activity as certain.

### lanes — per-semester ECTS straight off the lane headers, left to right as rendered
Format: `park<value>|<S1>/<S2>/.../<Sn>` — e.g. `park0.0|20.0/19.0/20.0/24.0`.
- Use `-` for a lane that is entirely out of the viewport this frame (not "probably
  the same as last frame" — literally not visible).
- Use `?` for a lane header that is present but unreadable (occluded by a banner/
  toolbar/panel edge, clipped at the viewport boundary, or too small even after
  crop+upscale). If a digit is partly visible, you may write e.g. `9.0?` and say why
  in `notes`.
- This field is `-` outright when `surface` is `graph` (the graph canvas renders no
  lane headers) — record plan state via `ects`/`miss`/`warn`/`focus` instead and say
  so once in `## GRAPH USE`.
- Lane order and count can change (Parking Stage always first if present; semester
  count follows the brief, typically 7 or 8). Keep the order stable within one chunk.

### ects — dashboard Planned ECTS / Target ECTS, e.g. `114.0/180.0`
Add `doneN.N` if the Done-mode total is also visible and non-zero, e.g.
`114.0/180.0 done0.0`. `-` if the Dashboard is closed or this tile isn't visible.
If Planned is legible but Target is scrolled out of view, write `114.0/?` and say in
`notes` which other on-screen total (Per Exam Subject / Per Category) you cross-checked
it against.

### miss — Missing Requirements count (integer), or `-` if the Dashboard is closed or
this row is unreadable. Include the requirement text in `notes` (or in the relevant
`## ANOMALIES`/`## FINAL STATE` entry) whenever the collapsible is expanded.

### warn — Warnings count (integer), or `-`. Same rule: text goes in `notes` if
expanded, and note explicitly if the count is visible but the panel is never expanded
in this chunk (so the text is never observed).

### focus — Focus Area status + checklist, e.g. `not completed 2/6` or
`Cybersecurity completed 6/6`. Include the focus-area name once it's established;
`-` if not visible.

### banner — transient toast/banner text this frame, verbatim (shortened if long,
but keep exact wording of the key clause), e.g. `accepted`, `rejected: semester 2
exceeds max load (31.0 ECTS > 27.0)`, or a milestone banner quoted in full in
`notes` if it's long. `-` if none fired this frame. Remember events under ~10s can
fall entirely between samples — you're recording what's on screen at the sample
instant, not reconstructing everything that happened.

### flags — comma-separated, any that apply; `-` if none
- `SWIM` — the **Per Semester (ECTS)** dashboard sub-panel is visibly *expanded*
  (not just the collapsed one-line summary), and you can read its actual verdict
  text. If only the collapsed summary line is ever shown for a whole chunk, say so
  once in `## ANOMALIES` rather than flagging SWIM on every frame.
- `FAC` — proxy for facilitator intervention: the webcam/video tile switches to the
  facilitator's name or to active-speaker on the facilitator. This is a visual proxy
  only, **not** transcript-corroborated — say so explicitly wherever you use it, and
  say so again as a caveat in `## ANOMALIES`.
- `XSTATE` — canvas and dashboard visibly disagree about plan state in this exact
  frame (e.g. a lane total that can't reconcile with the Planned-ECTS tile, or a
  rejection banner next to a dashboard message that contradicts it). Explain the
  disagreement in `notes` or in `## ANOMALIES`.
- `AXIS` — the bottom axis-config bar (`Horizontal: … | Vertical: …`) is visible
  this frame, **and/or** it just changed from the previous frame. Note the exact
  before/after strings in `## ANOMALIES` if it changed; if it's merely visible and
  unchanged, flagging it every frame is fine but say once, near the top of
  `## ANOMALIES`, that no edit was observed if that's the case.
- `GTIME` — the guide app's own session/task clock is visible this frame; record its
  reading in `notes`. Treat it as an independent, possibly-unreliable clock — see the
  freeze-detection guidance below.

### notes — free text, as dense as needed
This is where the actual observation lives: verbatim UI text (quote it), exact
course/module names, exact numbers, approximate cursor position in pixels when it's
informative, which video tile is showing, what changed since the previous frame,
and your reasoning for any `?`/`~` you used. Favour precision and direct quotation
(in backticks or bold) over paraphrase or summary. When two consecutive frames are
pixel-identical or near-identical, say so plainly ("identical to t-10") instead of
re-describing the whole screen.

## 4. Trailing sections

### `## ANOMALIES`
Numbered or lettered list. Each entry should be a specific, evidenced claim, not a
vague impression — cite the frame(s), quote the exact text, give the exact numbers.
Systematically check for and report on, at minimum:
- Any banner/message whose own numbers don't add up (e.g. a "25% complete" banner
  next to a total that isn't 25%).
- Any default/pre-filled value in a Setup or Profile modal that contradicts the
  brief's stated persona, and whether/when it was corrected.
- Any value set outside the brief's stated allowed band, and whether it looks
  mid-edit vs. committed (was the modal open on the *next* sampled frame too?).
- The full trajectory of every plan-state number that changed across the chunk
  (no decreases silently smoothed over — list them as `before → after` with the
  timestamps).
- Any warning/requirement count that is visible but whose contents are never
  actually shown because the collapsible was never expanded.
- Whether every semester lane the brief asks for was ever actually observed in
  viewport during this chunk — say plainly if a lane's presence/absence "cannot be
  established from this chunk" rather than assuming it exists off-screen.
- Every frame where the FAC proxy fired, listed together, with the caveat that it's
  unverified against transcript.
- Any participant-ID / naming mismatch between task framing, video tile, and app
  account — record it, don't resolve it.
- A consolidated list of every `?`/`-` you used and why, if there are more than a
  couple — so a reader can see at a glance what this chunk could and couldn't
  establish.
- Whether the Graph View toggle exists/is enabled at all in this chunk (relevant to
  confirming which experimental condition the run is actually in, independent of
  what the brief claims).
- Whether the axis configuration ever changed, and the exact strings either side.
- **Frozen-screen detection.** If a run of consecutive frames looks static, don't
  just call it "idle" — difference-check the frames (mentally or by direct
  pixel/region comparison if you have that tool available) and check whether the
  guide app's own clock (GTIME) is advancing at a real 1 Hz rate across that span.
  A clock that holds constant or jumps by more than the elapsed sample time means
  the screen share itself may be stalled — say so explicitly, and say that the
  block should not be read as measured idle time.
- Any layout/scale/geometry change in the captured surface itself (window resize,
  DPI change, cropped viewport) that isn't a UI event in the app.
- Any persistent visual artefact (stuck drag-ghost, leftover overlay) that could be
  mistaken for a real lane/card — describe its position and say explicitly that it
  carries no legible ECTS / isn't counted.

### `## BRIEF`
Quote the guide app's brief **verbatim**, from whichever frame(s) show it fully
(note if identical across all guide frames, or note the diff if it changes — e.g.
between the initial brief and a later confirmation screen). Preserve structure
(headings, bullets, bold/warning markers) using Markdown blockquote or similar.
Follow with two short summaries:
- **Extracted task parameters**, explicitly: number of semesters, target ECTS/
  semester, allowed band, any special-semester requirement (name it exactly as
  worded — "internship" and "exchange" are not interchangeable — and its ECTS cap),
  condition (graph enabled/disabled), and anything else numeric or constraint-like
  in the brief.
- **Setup / Profile values observed**, from whichever `auth`-surface or Profile-modal
  frames in this chunk show them: account name, programme (note if it changed and
  when), focus area, start term, graph-toggle state, Max ECTS/semester, Max
  Week-Hours/semester, Recommended ECTS, Recommended Week-Hours, Interests, Career
  Direction — mark any field never observed in this chunk as such rather than
  omitting it silently.

### `## GRAPH USE` (only if any frame has `surface = graph`)
State plainly whether Graph View was available and used, cross-checked against the
graph-toggle setting recorded under Setup/Profile. Then, per **episode** (a
contiguous span from entering to exiting Graph View):
- Entry/exit timestamps (bounded by the nearest sampled frames either side; state
  the sampled span you actually observed vs. the true entry/exit which may fall
  between samples).
- How the transition happened (clicked toggle; sidebar/axis-bar changes that come
  with it).
- What was done while in graph view (panning, node expansion order, which
  courses were toggled planned/not-planned) — be specific and chronological.
- Whether plan state (ects/miss/warn/focus) actually changed during the episode,
  with a `before → after` trajectory keyed to timestamps, or state plainly that it
  did not change and what was cross-checked to confirm that (since some dashboard
  tiles may be scrolled out of view).
- Explicitly note that lane-level (per-semester) totals are never visible in graph
  view, so any semester-level effect of a graph-view edit is only observable once
  Table view is re-entered — say when/whether that re-entry happens inside this
  chunk.
- Whether the session ends the chunk still inside graph view (i.e. never returns to
  Table view to review the finished plan).

### `## FINAL STATE` (only for the chunk that ends a run)
- Name the final planner frame (last `table`/`graph` frame, not final `guide`
  frame) and justify it.
- A full lane table: every semester + Parking Stage, its ECTS value (or `?`/`not in
  viewport` with the reason), and its card contents where legible — draw values from
  whichever nearby frames actually show each lane if the very last frame doesn't
  show all of them at once, and say which frame each figure came from.
- Show the sum of visible lanes against the dashboard's Planned-ECTS total, and say
  plainly if they don't fully reconcile (i.e. some ECTS sit in lanes never observed
  in viewport during the chunk) rather than assuming the difference away.
- A full dashboard readout at the final state: Program/Focus header, Target/
  Planned/Done ECTS, StEOP, Focus Area + checklist, Per Exam Subject, Per Semester
  (with its collapsed/expanded state), Estimated Week-Hours, Per Category, Missing
  Requirements (with text if expanded), Warnings (with text if expanded), axis bar.
- A **measured, not judged** compliance check against the brief's own stated
  numbers (semester count, target band per semester, special-semester cap, overall
  target) — report the numbers and which specific semesters/values are in or out
  of band; do not characterise this as good/bad performance, just state the facts
  the brief itself put on the table.

## 5. What this is not

- Not a synthesis or usability judgement. Don't diagnose *why* something happened,
  don't rate the interface, don't editorialise about the participant. Describe what
  the frame shows, with numbers and quotes.
- Not a transcript coding. If you don't have the audio, don't claim to know what was
  said — FAC is a video-tile proxy and must be labelled as such every time it's used.
- Not a smoothed or resampled series. A `?` or `-` is a correct, useful answer; a
  guessed number is not. If you truly cannot resolve a value even after
  crop/zoom, leave it unresolved and say so — do not average, interpolate, or infer
  it from a neighbouring frame.
